## The Measurement Trap

Modern supply chains generate infinite data. Every transaction, movement, status change is logged, timestamped, stored. Dashboards proliferate. Metrics multiply. Reports accumulate.

None of this guarantees insight.

Most organizations measure what is easy, not what matters. They track activity, not outcomes. They optimize local metrics, not system performance. They drown in data and thirst for wisdom.

## The Hierarchy of Metrics

Not all numbers deserve equal attention. The effective framework distinguishes levels:

**Strategic metrics** — Board and CEO focus. Cash conversion cycle. Working capital as percent of revenue. Supply chain cost to serve. These determine enterprise value.

**Tactical metrics** — Executive and VP focus. Inventory turns by category. Perfect order rate. Supplier performance scorecard. These guide resource allocation and priority.

**Operational metrics** — Manager and supervisor focus. Pick accuracy. On-time shipment. Dock-to-stock time. These drive daily execution and improvement.

The error is confusing levels. Operational metrics reported to the board. Strategic metrics ignored by operations. Misalignment produces noise, not signal.

## Vanity vs. Action Metrics

| Vanity Metric | Action Metric | Why It Matters |
|-------------|-------------|--------------|
| Total inventory value | Inventory turns by velocity segment | Turns drive cash flow, not absolute value |
| On-time delivery percentage | Cost of late delivery by customer | Not all lateness is equal |
| Supplier count | Supplier performance distribution | Fewer excellent beats many mediocre |
| Transportation spend per unit | Total landed cost by source | Cheaper freight can increase total cost |
| Forecast accuracy | Forecast value add | Perfect forecast of wrong demand is useless |

## The Cost of Measurement

Every metric tracked has a cost. Data collection, system integration, reporting, review time. The burden compounds.

The disciplined question: What decision does this metric inform? If none, eliminate it. If unclear, clarify or remove.

The best organizations measure less, not more. They focus on the critical few that predict outcomes and guide action.

## The Lead Indicator Problem

Most metrics are lagging. They report what happened. They do not predict what will happen.

**Lagging indicators** — inventory level, stockout frequency, cost per unit. Outcomes already determined.

**Leading indicators** — supplier lead time trend, forecast bias, capacity utilization trajectory. Early warnings of future outcomes.

The shift to predictive measurement requires:
- Understanding causal relationships
- Investing in data infrastructure
- Tolerating uncertainty and false signals
- Building organizational trust in predictions

## The GM Experience

At General Motors, we built KPI frameworks across 15 countries, 700+ suppliers, $7B freight spend. The learning:

**Start with decisions.** What must we decide weekly, monthly, quarterly? Design metrics to inform those decisions.

**Segment ruthlessly.** A-items deserve detailed tracking. C-items get aggregate treatment. Measurement intensity matches business impact.

**Visualize exception.** Green metrics require no attention. Red metrics demand action. Dashboards should surface only what needs eyes.

**Review and retire.** Every metric has a half-life. Business changes, metrics become irrelevant. Quarterly audit: keep, modify, eliminate.

## The Microsoft Perspective

At Microsoft, we measured pre-sales effectiveness through pipeline velocity, win rates, deal size, time-to-close. The metrics drove behavior: pursue larger deals, accelerate early engagement, disqualify unlikely opportunities.

The same discipline applies to supply chain. Metrics shape behavior. Design carefully, because people optimize what is measured.

## The Three Metrics That Matter

If forced to three, the choice depends on strategic priority:

**Cash-focused organization:**
- Cash conversion cycle (strategic)
- Inventory turns (tactical)
- Days payable outstanding (operational)

**Service-focused organization:**
- Perfect order rate (strategic)
- On-time-in-full (tactical)
- Order cycle time (operational)

**Cost-focused organization:**
- Total cost to serve (strategic)
- Cost per unit by channel (tactical)
- Productivity per labor hour (operational)

No universal answer. The right metrics align with strategy and drive the decisions that execute it.

## The Bottom Line

Quantification is essential. Measurement is dangerous without discipline.

The effective supply chain organization:
- Measures what drives decisions, not what is easy to count
- Distinguishes strategic, tactical, operational levels
- Balances lagging outcomes with leading predictions
- Reviews and retires metrics as business evolves
- Recognizes that every metric tracked has a cost in attention and action

The goal is not more data. It is better judgment, informed by the right data.

&gt; In God we trust. All others bring data. But bring the right data, or drown in numbers that decide nothing.

